  "scope": "Memory and durable group finalization inside native branch settlement: completed replay, sequential stop propagation, pending-member gates, merge/successor routing and terminal publication. Original merge/binding evaluation, native queries/codecs/quota/writes and outer commit ownership remain unchanged.",
  "limitations": [
    "Frozen finalization bodies share storage, codecs, reducers and the original merge/successor evaluator, not migrated finalization coordination.",
    "The 2240 memory cases span 70 operations, two execution orders and 16 low-level scenarios; they do not claim all operation-specific host results are covered.",
    "The 128 SQLite cases span four source group shapes and 32 scenarios, not all 70 operations. Corrupt fixtures temporarily disable constraints for setup and restore them before invoking production code.",
    "Memory retains completed-group bypass, lazy stop reads, missing/acknowledged-member behavior and mutations before a late error. No memory rollback or new recovery repair policy is introduced.",
    "SQLite retains completed receipt validation before plan reads, branch-query errors before plan decoding, eager sequential byte decoding before stop selection and pending-member masking of later invalid state.",
    "Twelve memory and twelve durable bound shapes reuse the existing evaluator through initial merge, tail/capture/dataflow successor insertion and final receipt; this is not a second standalone evaluator.",
    "Eight public last-member cases use update and deferred-commit failure triggers, reopen and explicit retry; outer transactions roll back branch and successor writes and no completed cache entry is published.",
    "Direct callback fault/unwind tests assert no retries or later calls; they do not simulate native memory rollback. Native SQLite owns atomicity and the borrowed finalization adapter cannot commit.",
    "No new Linux, remote CI, live GUI, standalone exported-source execution, power-loss, disk-full, multi-host race, throughput benchmark or full host-neutral durable recovery is claimed."
  ],
